44
00:24:36.000 --> 00:25:01.000
who usually shows up in the historical record. Julia Sand is 31, unmarried, chronically ill, and confined to her home. She's a woman in an era when women have no political voice. She cannot vote. She has no platform or power or influence. She's invisible in every way that. matters in her time. But from that invisibility, she picks up a pen and writes to the vice president,
就在这时，他收到了这封开始改变他人生的信。那是 1881 年 8 月。亚瑟收到了来自朱莉娅·桑的第一封信，而她绝不是那种通常会出现在历史记录里的人。朱莉娅·桑 31 岁，未婚，长期患病，足不出户。在女性没有政治发声渠道的时代，她是一个女性。她没有投票权。她没有平台、权力和影响力。在对她那个时代重要的任何方面，她都是隐形的。但正是从这种隐形状态中，她拿起笔给副总统写了一封信，

45
00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:53.000
a letter so politically astute, so beguiling, that it will transform him. And she comes in hot. August 27, 1881, to the Honorable Chester A. Arthur, the hours of Garfield's life are numbered. Before this meets your eye, you may be president. The people are bowed and in grief. But do you realize it? Not so much because he's dying as because you are his successor. And she talks about everyone is sure you will be a terrible president. But then she says, making a man president can change him. And she says, here's the thing you have to do. Reform. Rise to the emergency. Disappoint our fears. Force the nation to have faith in you. Show them from the first that you have none but the purest aims.
一封在政治上如此精明、如此迷人，以至于将改变他的信。她一上来就单刀直入。1881 年 8 月 27 日，致尊敬的切斯特·A·亚瑟阁下：加菲尔德的生命已所剩无几。在您看到这封信之前，您可能已经成为总统了。人们正黯然神伤。但您意识到了吗？这并非因为他濒临死亡，而是因为您将是他的继任者。她谈到每个人都确信你会是一个糟糕的总统。但接着她说：让一个人当总统可以改变他。她说，这是你必须要做的事情：改革。迎难而上。打破我们的担忧。迫使国家对你产生信心。从一开始就向他们展示你除了最纯粹的目标之外别无他求。

46
00:25:53.000 --> 00:26:21.000
It may be difficult to inspire confidence, but persevere. In time, when you have given reason for it, the country will love and trust you. Sands' letter goes beyond just encouragement. It's a challenge. She picks one thing, one issue, and she makes it Arthur's mission. Civil Service Reform. If any man says with Arthur for president's civil service reform is doomed, prove that Arthur can be its finest champion.
要激发信心或许很难，但要坚持下去。假以时日，当你给出了理由，国家将会爱戴并信任你。桑的信不仅仅是鼓励。它是一个挑战。她挑选了一件事、一个议题，并将其变成了亚瑟的使命：公务员制度改革。如果有人说有亚瑟当总统，公务员制度改革就彻底无望了，那就证明亚瑟可以成为它最好的捍卫者。

47
00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:40.000
She is a single-issue voter in this one. It is civil service reform. You can do it. Civil service reform. Civil service reform. And she makes it clear. This is how you prove you're not the man everyone thinks you are. I wish we knew more about why she decided to write this letter, why she had any faith in Arthur to begin with.
她在这个问题上是一个只关注单一议题的选民。这就是公务员制度改革。你能做到的。公务员制度改革。公务员制度改革。她明确指出：这就是你证明自己不是每个人所想的那种人的方式。我真希望我们能对她为什么决定写这封信、她为什么一开始对亚瑟有信心了解更多。

48
00:26:40.000 --> 00:27:05.000
We just don't know. And all we can pick up are the clues from this letter and the 22 others that she writes after that, where she starts establishing herself as what she calls his dwarf saying that in the court, of kings, the dwarf was allowed to say the things that other people couldn't say, and to give the king his honest opinion. It's brilliant. She has no power, no influence, no formal standing. But she uses that powerlessness as a kind of power.
我们只是不得而知。我们所能捕捉到的，只有这封信以及她后来写的另外 22 封信中的线索，在那些信中，她开始确立自己作为她所谓的他的“侏儒”的身份，并说在国王的宫廷里，侏儒被允许说其他人不敢说的话，并向国王提供诚实的意见。这太绝了。她没有权力、没有影响力、没有正式地位。但她把这种无权无势变成了一种力量。

49
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:27.000
So she's writing to him saying, you have this decision for you. I think you should do it this way. Don't do it that way. Here's what I think you should do. But written also in a way that is frankly, like, really delightful, like very playful. She teases him often in one of the letters she talks about, she's like, oh, I saw it. Did you hear I saw a picture of that awful. President Arthur in the newspaper? He had devil horns, I think.
所以她写信给他：你面临这个决定，我认为你应该这么做。不要那样做。这是我认为你该做的。但这些信的语气坦白说也写得非常令人愉快、非常俏皮。她经常在信中取笑他，比如她在其中一封信里谈到，她会说：哦，我看到了。你听说了吗？我在报纸上看到了那个可怕的亚瑟总统的照片。我想他头上长了恶魔的角。

50
00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:57.000
Like, she's very playful with him, but also very stern. And taking a tone with him that feels very presumptuous in that, again, she's writing to, at first, the man who is going to be president. And then to the president. In subsequent letters, Julia Sands' advice covers everything. By the way, do you take care of your health? Perhaps naturally it is good, but there are limits to human strength. She tells him to rest properly, to rise. horseback daily for circulation and health.
比如，她和他开玩笑时非常调皮，但同时也非常严厉。她对他采取的语气显得非常冒昧，因为再次强调，她一开始是写给即将成为总统的人。然后是写给总统。在随后的信件中，朱莉娅·桑的建议涵盖了方方面面。顺便问一句，你照顾好自己的健康了吗？也许你天生体质好，但人的体力是有限的。她告诉他要好好休息，每天骑马去促进血液循环和保持健康。

51
00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:29.000
Nothing supplies the place of fresh air and sunshine. But she also tells him to stay away from New York and his old stalwart buddies, men like Roscoe Conklin, whose spoils machine made him rich. New York is the one spot on the continent where you positively ought not to be this fall. In coming to New York at this season, you do. You put yourself in temptation. And in a certain way, you put yourself in the power of unconstitutional. untrustworthy men. She pushes him to appoint qualified people, not political cronies, to stop the
没有什么能替代新鲜空气和阳光。但她也告诉他要远离纽约和他的老坚守派密友，比如罗斯科·康克林这样的人，正是他的分赃机器让他发了财。纽约是这个大陆上你今年秋天绝对不应该去的地方。在这个季节来到纽约，你是在自找诱惑。某种程度上，你把自己置于那些违宪且不值得信任的人的权力之下。她敦促他任命合格的人选，而不是政治亲信，停止

52
