kickbacks or assessments to the party. Appoint those only of marked ability and of sterling character. Such may not be abundant, but you will find them if you seek them. To think about his legacy, about how history will judge him, about his children watching. Of all your friends, do you know which ones I like best to hear of being with you? your children, they are your guardian angels. Over and over, across the 23 letters, the message is the same. Do better, because you can be better. And Arthur actually listens.
向党组织输送利益或摊派。只任命那些能力显著、品格纯良的人。这样的人或许并不多，但只要你用心寻找，就一定能找到。去想想他的政治遗产，想想历史会如何评判他，想想他的孩子们正在看着他。在你所有的朋友中，你知道我最喜欢听哪一个陪伴在你的左右吗？是你的孩子们，他们就是你的守护天使。在这23封信中，核心信息一遍又一遍地重复着：做得更好，因为你有能力做得更好。亚瑟真的听进去了。

53
00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:43.000
He doesn't just read her words. He acts on them. When he vetoes the River and Harbor Act of 1882, Julius Sand is genuinely delighted. Arthur says to Congress that it was filled with bogus local projects that were not in the national interest, writing it would set a bad precedent for the, quote, extravagant expenditure of public money. His veto was eventually overridden by Congress, but Julius Sand was genuinely satisfied with President Arthur's choice. She writes to congratulate him, saying she sees it as proof that he can do the right thing.
他不仅是在读她的信，更是付诸行动。当他在1882年否决《河流与港口法案》时，朱莉娅·桑是由衷地感到高兴。亚瑟对国会表示，该法案充斥着不符合国家利益的虚假地方项目，并写道，此举将为所谓的“公共资金的挥霍”开创一个糟糕的先例。尽管他的否决最终被国会推翻，但朱莉娅·桑对阿瑟总统的选择感到由衷的满意。她写信向他祝贺，称这证明了他能够做出正确的事情。

54
00:29:43.000 --> 00:30:23.000
I want to complicate this, like, slightly in the fact that this didn't mean that Chester Arthur did all great fans in his presidency. I think he did really contemptible things as president, too. In Arthur's case, the worst of it was he did sign into law the Chinese Exclusion Act, which basically said Chinese people are not welcome in the United States for a certain period of time. In her letters, Julius and advised President Arthur to veto the Chinese Exclusion Act, because, as she put it, it is opposed to the spirit of America and to the spirit of civilization all around the world. It is mean and cowardly. More than that, it is a step back into barbarism.
我想稍微辩证地看待这一点，事实上，这并不意味着切斯特·亚瑟在他总统任内的所有举措都堪称伟大。我认为他在总统任上也做过一些确实令人不齿的事情。就亚瑟而言，其中最糟糕的一点就是他签署了《排华法案》，该法案基本上规定在一定时期内不欢迎中国人在美国居留。朱莉娅·桑在信中曾建议亚瑟总统否决《排华法案》，因为正如她所说，这违背了美国的精神，违背了全世界文明的精神。这是卑鄙而懦弱的。不仅如此，这是向野蛮主义的倒退。

55
00:30:23.000 --> 00:31:15.000
At all events, do not let your administration be marked by any such disgraceful retrograde movements. Sand in her letters is saying, you've got to veto that bill. And he vetoes it the first time, but then it comes back to him, it's still a racist anti-immigration act, that singles out a specific ethnicity and nationality for not being allowed in the United States. And he realizes that they have the votes. to overcome his veto. And so he, I think, reluctantly signs it. And Julia Sand is frustrated with him about this. But despite spending his career benefiting from the spoil system, despite once being its patron saint, he begins to reform. In 1883, he signs the Pendleton Civil Service Reform Act. It's the first law that fundamentally changes how government works.
无论如何，绝不能让你的政府留下任何此类可耻的倒退行径。桑在信中说，你必须否决那个法案。他第一次确实将其否决了，但随后法案又回到了他的桌面上——这仍然是一项针对特定种族和国籍、禁止其进入美国的种族主义反移民法案。他意识到国会有足够的票数来推翻他的否决。因此，我认为他是不情愿地签署了它。朱莉娅·桑对此感到很沮丧。但尽管他的整个职业生涯都受益于分赃制，尽管他曾是这一制度的守护神，他还是开始推行改革。1883年，他签署了《彭德尔顿公务员制度改革法》。这是第一部从根本上改变政府运作方式的法律。

56
00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:54.000
Certain federal jobs are now awarded based on merit, not political loyalty. The set group of jobs in the federal government, they're going to be awarded on the basis of merit, not on the basis of patronage, and they will not be turned over jobs. That you won't have this revolving door of a new government came in. We're going to kick everybody out. It's really the establishment of the permanent federal bureaucracy. You know, like allies don't just like take over. And because you donated a bunch of money, you become the tax collector or whatever. Like it is making those jobs permanent and consistent across. administration so that the job of government is permanent and consistent.
某些联邦职位现在开始根据能力而非政治忠诚度来授予。联邦政府中的特定职位，将根据能力而非裙带关系来分配，并且这些职位将不再随政权更迭而被撤换。你不会再看到新政府上台后，出现这种走马灯式的更迭。我们要把所有人赶出去。这实际上就是永久性联邦官僚机构的确立。你知道的，就好比盟友不会直接接管一切。不会因为你捐了一大笔钱，你就成了税务官之类的人。这就好比让这些职位在各届政府中保持永久性和连贯性，从而使政府的职能得以永久且稳定地运行。

57
00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:36.000
Yes. And so you have, it's the very beginning of building up the institutional knowledge of how a government functions. The thing that is in such danger right now, because of the way that the current administration is handling, employing in the federal government, this is the beginning of that. And Arthur becomes a real champion for this act. Now civil servants are protected. They can't be fired just because a new president takes office. It's a complete reversal of the system Arthur built his career on. The guy they put on the ticket to appeal to champions of the spoil system has dismantled the system himself. The reformers have won the battle.
没错。所以这就成为了积累政府职能制度知识的最初起点。而鉴于现任政府在联邦政府雇佣问题上的处理方式，这种制度目前正处于极大的危险之中，这一切正是从那时开始的。亚瑟成为了这项法案的坚定捍卫者。现在，公务员得到了保护。他们不会仅仅因为新总统上台而被解雇。这是对亚瑟赖以起家的那个制度的彻底颠覆。那个被他们推上候选人宝座、用来迎合分赃制拥趸的人，竟然亲手拆解了这个制度。改革派赢得了这场战役。

58
00:32:36.000 --> 00:33:10.000
I'm wondering if you know how his old cronies with the spoil system responded to his sort of about-face when he signed the Pendleton Act and championed it. He had a break with Conkling. I mean, Conkling also, he flamed out spectacularly at a certain point where he was mad about something. And he resigned his seat in the Senate. And he's like, you'll see, I'm so powerful, the legislature's going to send me right back. Because at that point, the legislature elected senators, people didn't direct elect senators. And the legislature was like, uh, thank you for giving us this opportunity to get rid of you. And so he flames out of government amazingly.
我想知道你是否了解，当亚瑟签署并大力推行《彭德尔顿法案》时，他在分赃制阵营中的那些老政客伙伴们作何反应。他与康克林决裂了。我是说，康克林在某个时候也因为对某些事感到不满而彻底崩溃了。他辞去了参议员职务。他当时的想法是：你们看好了，我是如此有权有势，立法机关马上就会把我重新选回去。因为在那个时候，参议员是由立法机关选举产生的，而不是由民众直接选举产生的。而立法机关的反应则是：呃，谢谢你给了我们这个摆脱你的好机会。于是，他在政坛上的政治生命以一种惊人的方式彻底黯然收场。

59
00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:36.000
It's reported that Conkling then asks Arthur to make some typical spoil system appointments, but Arthur refuses. And when pressed on why he turned on his old mentor, Arthur says, quote, for the vice president, I was indebted to Mr. Conklin. But for the presidency of the United States, my debt is to the Almighty. That was it. The friendship was over. He cut ties with his spoil system buddies in New York,
据报道，康克林随后要求亚瑟进行一些典型的分赃制人事任命，但遭到亚瑟的拒绝。当被追问为什么要背叛自己的老导师时，亚瑟回答说：“关于副总统之位，我亏欠了康克林先生。但对于美利坚合众国的总统之位，我所亏欠的是全能的上帝。”事情就是这样。这段友谊结束了。他切断了与纽约分赃制死党们的联系，

